program metered_billing

-- ============================================================
-- Metered billing: pricing measured consumption, to the penny
-- ============================================================
--
-- A metered supply - electricity, gas, water - is billed in three
-- moves. A meter measures how much was used: the volume, in kilowatt
-- hours here. A tariff prices each unit, quoted in pence per kWh the
-- way GB energy tariffs are. And the bill settles in pounds, where a
-- payable amount has exactly two decimal places. Between the meter
-- and the bill sits an awkward piece of arithmetic: rate times
-- volume almost never lands on a whole penny. 13.5 pence times 431.7 kWh is 58.2795
-- pounds - and nobody can pay 58.2795 pounds. Someone must decide
-- what the payable figure is, and with money involved, "roughly" is
-- a dispute waiting to happen.
--
-- The decision is a rounding convention. This programme makes the
-- convention a law of the ledger rather than a habit of the billing
-- engine. The engine still computes the figures - that is its job -
-- but every figure it proposes is recomputed and compared by a rule
-- the runtime enforces, and a figure that disagrees, by even a
-- penny, cannot be admitted at all. Not by a buggy engine, not by a
-- hand-edited correction, not by anyone.
--
-- The convention itself, spelled once and enforced everywhere:
-- round to the NEAREST penny, and when the fraction is an exact
-- half - 0.125 pounds, dead between 12p and 13p - round AWAY FROM
-- ZERO, so 0.125 becomes 0.13 and a credit of -0.125 becomes -0.13.
-- That symmetry matters: a convention that always rounded halves UP
-- would quietly favour one side of every credit note.

-- One figure the whole rulebook shares: the penny, the quantum every
-- money amount rounds to. A `const` names it once at the top of the
-- file and is written out in place before the rules take effect -
-- nothing is stored, and naming the figure provably cannot change
-- what any rule means. What it buys is agreement: two rules can no
-- longer round to DIFFERENT quanta by a typo one of them carries.
const penny = (0.01)

-- A declared VAT rate. Tax rates are law, not arithmetic: a rate is
-- admitted here as a claim with its own identity, and every line
-- must name the rate it was taxed under. `0.05` is the GB reduced
-- rate most domestic energy carries; `rate` is a plain fraction.
predicate VatRate(rate_id: Subject, rate: Decimal)
    unique by (rate_id)

-- One line of an invoice: a volume priced by a rate, with the net
-- and VAT amounts the engine computed. The names carry the units -
-- pence per kWh in, pounds out - which is the bare-decimal money
-- convention: the numbers are exact decimals, and the field name
-- says what they denominate. With seven fields, the rules below
-- match lines by NAMING the fields they read - ChargeLine(invoice:
-- invoice, net_gbp: n, ..) - and the `..` leaves the rest
-- unmatched, so no one counts underscores and a rule survives the
-- line growing a field.
predicate ChargeLine(
    line: Subject,
    invoice: Subject,
    rate_p_per_kwh: Decimal,
    volume_kwh: Decimal,
    net_gbp: Decimal,
    vat_rate_id: Subject,
    vat_gbp: Decimal,
) unique by (line)

-- An invoice sealed at a total. Sealing is the moment the figure
-- becomes the official ask; after it, the line set is closed.
predicate InvoiceSealed(invoice: Subject, total_gbp: Decimal)
    unique by (invoice)

-- The rules. An invariant is a rule the runtime will not let any
-- change break - not a check the engine is asked to run, but a
-- property the ledger cannot leave.

-- Every line's net IS the rounded recompute. `let` names the raw
-- product - pence times kilowatt hours, over 100 to reach pounds -
-- and `round(raw, penny)` is the convention: nearest penny, halves
-- away from zero. The engine's figure is not trusted; it is checked
-- against this recompute on every admission, forever.
--
-- `line` and `invoice` are named rather than left as `_`, and nothing
-- in the arithmetic uses them. They are here for the refusal: when this
-- rule stops a bill, it can only report the values it named, so writing
-- `_` would leave a biller reading "some line is wrong" with a whole
-- invoice to search. What a rule binds decides how good its refusals are.
invariant line_net_is_the_rounded_recompute:
    let raw = ((rate_p_per_kwh * volume_kwh) / 100)
    ChargeLine(line, invoice, rate_p_per_kwh, volume_kwh, net_gbp, _, _)
    implies net_gbp = round(raw, penny)

-- Every line's VAT is the rounded recompute from the declared rate
-- it names. Note what this rule alone cannot see: if a line named a
-- rate nobody declared, there would be no VatRate claim to compare
-- against, and this rule would pass EMPTILY - a wrong VAT amount
-- sailing through because the comparison never happened. Rules that
-- select a governing claim go quiet when none exists.
invariant line_vat_is_the_rounded_recompute:
    (ChargeLine(net_gbp: net_gbp, vat_rate_id: vat_rate_id, vat_gbp: vat_gbp, ..) and VatRate(vat_rate_id, rate))
    implies vat_gbp = round(net_gbp * rate, penny)

-- ... which is why this companion exists: every line MUST name a
-- declared rate. The two rules only govern together - the one above
-- checks the arithmetic when the rate exists, this one guarantees
-- the rate exists. Drop this rule and the one above becomes a
-- promise that holds only on the happy path.
invariant every_line_names_a_declared_rate:
    ChargeLine(vat_rate_id: vat_rate_id, ..) implies VatRate(vat_rate_id, _)

-- The inputs live in their lawful ranges: a VAT rate is a fraction
-- of the net, and a metered volume or tariff rate is never negative
-- here. Without these two rules the model would quietly accept a
-- negative line - a credit note by accident, independently rounded.
-- Credits are a real thing, but they are a DIFFERENT model (an
-- amount copy-negated from the committed line it reverses, never
-- re-rounded), and a boundary a programme only describes in prose is
-- not a boundary. These rules close it.
invariant vat_rate_is_a_fraction:
    VatRate(_, rate) implies 0 <= rate <= 1

invariant charge_inputs_are_non_negative:
    ChargeLine(rate_p_per_kwh: rate_p_per_kwh, volume_kwh: volume_kwh, ..)
    implies (0 <= rate_p_per_kwh and 0 <= volume_kwh)

-- A sealed total is the sum of its rounded lines - net plus VAT,
-- line by line. This pins the OTHER half of the convention: round
-- per line, THEN sum. The rival reading - sum the raw figures, round
-- once at the end - genuinely differs: two lines of 0.4 pence each
-- round to 0.00 + 0.00 = 0.00 per-line, but 0.008 pounds rounded
-- once is 0.01. Neither is more "correct"; they are different
-- contracts. This ledger's contract is per-line, and a total
-- computed the other way is refused, one penny of drift and all.
invariant sealed_total_is_the_sum_of_its_lines:
    let net_total = (sum(n | ChargeLine(invoice: invoice, net_gbp: n, ..)))
    let vat_total = (sum(v | ChargeLine(invoice: invoice, vat_gbp: v, ..)))
    InvoiceSealed(invoice, total_gbp) implies total_gbp = net_total + vat_total

-- The only ways state may change.

-- Tax law arrives before any billing: a rate is declared once under
-- its own identity.
transformation declare_vat_rate(rate_id, rate):
    require not VatRate(rate_id, _)
    admit VatRate(rate_id, rate)

-- The engine proposes a fully-computed line. The runtime does not
-- compute the bill FOR the engine - it recomputes what the engine
-- claims and refuses disagreement, which is the whole control:
-- computation and enforcement in different hands. A line can only
-- join an invoice that is not yet sealed.
transformation add_charge_line(line, invoice, rate_p_per_kwh, volume_kwh, net_gbp, vat_rate_id, vat_gbp):
    require not InvoiceSealed(invoice, _)
    admit ChargeLine(line, invoice, rate_p_per_kwh, volume_kwh, net_gbp, vat_rate_id, vat_gbp)

-- Sealing closes the invoice at a total. The gate demands at least
-- one line; the invariant above demands the total be exactly the sum
-- of the rounded lines.
transformation seal_invoice(invoice, total_gbp):
    require ChargeLine(invoice: invoice, ..)
    require not InvoiceSealed(invoice, _)
    admit InvoiceSealed(invoice, total_gbp)

-- What this example deliberately does not cover: credit notes and
-- reversals (amounts copy-negate the committed line, never re-round
-- - a modelling rule this gallery pins elsewhere; the non-negative
-- rules above make the exclusion enforced, not just stated),
-- corrections after sealing (supersession, as in the verified-
-- revenue example), and period assignment for the volumes. One
-- convention, made law, is the point.
